l well kids  will think that Anna is simply making a  cute reference to Kristof's regular  leather jerkin their parents will get  the joke lights go out on I like to get  a little freaky this is awkward that's  not the only racy comment in the scene  either after the exchange Olaf who  similarly dressed up remarks I'm shocked  you can last an hour and if you don't  get that one well ask your parents maybe  Hamilton and Glee star Jonathan Groff  who voices Kristoff didn't get a solo  song in the original frozen but frozen  to made it up for him in the sequel  Groff belts out lost in the woods one of  the film's most memorable tracks sure  Groff's performance is unsurprisingly  spectacular but what really makes lost  in the woods shine is its product  the number doesn't just evoke 80s power  ballads it's also staged exactly like  something you would have seen on classic  MTV with a few shots ripped directly  from Queen's Bohemian Rhapsody thrown in  for good measure  the whole thing is outlandish enough to  be funny for everyone but only adults  will understand what lost in the woods  is parodying that's by design in an  interview with Entertainment Weekly  Groff says it's really kind of a gift  for the adults watching the movie the  kids are laughing too but the adults are  the ones that are really in on the joke  yes it's true in frozen - Elsa and Anna  wear pants as co-director Jennifer Lee  points out that makes sense as the  filmmaker put it - the Los Angeles Times  of course you're going to wear pants  when you hear the words enchanted forest  frozen - is a rough-and-tumble adventure  and it's heroes are princesses they  should be allowed to wear whatever they  want and yet over the course of Disney's  history that hasn't always been the case  aside from Mulan and Aladdin's Princess  Jasmine every other Disney princess has  been confined to dresses or skirts even  Anna and Elsa were stuck in long flowing  gowns in the first frozen and only got  to wear more prac